# changelog

# Conduit v3.0.0

Conduit v3 marks the completion of the new isolated-session architecture and turns Conduit into a complete install-and-run tool rather than a standalone network namespace script.

## Architecture

- Every application launch now receives its own isolated Linux network namespace
- Every session has its own WireGuard interface, routes, DNS configuration, state, and lifecycle
- Multiple Conduit sessions can run simultaneously without affecting each other
- Different applications can use different VPN providers and profiles at the same time
- Temporary host-side WireGuard interfaces use unique names to avoid concurrent launch collisions
- WireGuard interfaces are renamed to `wg0` after entering their destination namespace
- The host routing table remains untouched

## Session management

- GUI applications now launch detached from the terminal by default
- Interactive shells remain foreground by default
- Added `-d` / `--detach`
- Added `-f` / `--foreground`
- Added `conduit status`
- Added `conduit attach`
- Added `conduit attach <session> <command>`
- Added `conduit logs <session>`
- Added `conduit kill <session>`
- Added `conduit kill --all`
- Sessions can be selected using their full ID, unique ID prefix, application name, or profile
- Detached sessions use a lifecycle supervisor so closing the launching terminal does not terminate the VPN session
- Session cleanup waits for application descendants that survive their original launcher process

## VPN and profile support

- Added Cloudflare WARP WireGuard support
- Added automatic Cloudflare WARP profile bootstrap when no VPN profiles exist
- Added `conduit bootstrap`
- WARP WireGuard keypairs are generated locally
- Generated WARP profiles are stored using secure `0600` permissions
- Added dual-stack IPv4/IPv6 routing
- Added support for multiple comma-separated interface addresses
- Added support for multiple IPv4 and IPv6 DNS servers
- WireGuard profile MTU values are respected
- Cloudflare WARP `MTU = 1280` configurations are supported
- Added provider directory layout:

```text
~/vpns/proton/
~/vpns/mullvad/
~/vpns/cloudflare/
```

- Arbitrary provider directories are supported
- Legacy flat Proton/Mullvad profile layouts remain supported
- Random profile selection avoids the previously used profile when possible
- VPN configuration files are validated for secure ownership and permissions

## DNS

- Added per-network-namespace DNS configuration
- Each session receives its own `resolv.conf`
- Each session receives a namespace-specific `nsswitch.conf`
- Host-local resolver integrations are prevented from bypassing the VPN DNS path
- Host resolver configuration is not modified

## Desktop integration

- Removed KDE/Plasma-specific session discovery
- Desktop environment variables are captured generically before privilege escalation
- Added support for Wayland and X11 session variables
- Added PipeWire, PulseAudio, D-Bus, portal, Qt, GTK, SDL, and common desktop environment variables
- Conduit no longer depends on a running `plasmashell` process

## Privilege handling

- Removed the previous `SETENV` sudo requirement
- Conduit now passes only a restricted allowlist of desktop environment variables
- Applications are dropped back to the invoking desktop user before execution
- `setpriv` is preferred with `runuser` available as a fallback
- Non-interactive privilege escalation prevents detached applications from hanging on password prompts

## Diagnostics

- Added `conduit doctor`
- Added dependency diagnostics
- Added profile ownership and permission checks
- Added network namespace creation test
- Added WireGuard kernel interface test
- Added desktop session detection
- Added privilege escalation diagnostics
- Added `conduit --version`

## Installation

- Added distro-agnostic `install.sh`
- Installer validates Bash syntax before installation
- Installer checks required capabilities instead of detecting distributions
- Installer installs `/usr/local/bin/conduit` as a root-owned executable
- Installer creates and validates `/etc/sudoers.d/conduit`
- Installer prepares the profile directory securely
- Installer automatically bootstraps Cloudflare WARP when no profiles exist
- Installer runs `conduit doctor` after installation
- Installation failures are designed to roll back system-level changes

## Uninstallation

- Added `uninstall.sh`
- Active Conduit sessions are stopped before removal
- Conduit-owned network namespaces and runtime state are cleaned up
- The installed binary and sudoers rule are removed
- User VPN profiles are deliberately preserved

## Breaking changes from the original architecture

The old design used a single shared `conduit` network namespace.

Conduit v3 replaces it with independent per-session namespaces such as:

```text
conduit-1000-a13f3c21
conduit-1000-b82e21ac
```

Closing one application no longer tears down unrelated Conduit applications.

Commands based on the old global-tunnel lifecycle should be replaced with the new session management interface:

```bash
conduit status
conduit attach <session>
conduit logs <session>
conduit kill <session>
conduit kill --all
```

This release supersedes the original single-namespace Conduit design.